---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-spain`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Spain',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Spain', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Spain: The Mediterranean Paradox Explained"
  description="Sunny Spain has surprisingly high vitamin D deficiency — the 'Mediterranean paradox.' Why latitude, midday-avoidance, skin tone, and indoor life combine to lower levels."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-spain", label: "Vitamin D in Spain" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Spain</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Spain is the poster child for what researchers call the "Mediterranean paradox": a sun-drenched
      country where vitamin D deficiency is nonetheless common — often <em>more</em> common than in
      cloudier, higher-latitude northern Europe. It seems to defy sense until you look at the details of
      latitude, skin, and daily habits.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The latitude surprise</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Mainland Spain lies at roughly 36–43°N — further north than many people assume, and on a par with
      the northern United States. Above about 35°N, the winter sun sits too low for effective UVB for
      part of the year, so Spain does have a vitamin D winter, especially in the north. The abundant
      Spanish sunshine is real, but much of it is either too low-angle (winter) or arrives at hours when
      people deliberately stay out of it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Behaviour and skin</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Spanish culture famously avoids the fierce midday sun — the siesta and late schedule keep people
      indoors during the very hours when UVB is strongest. Southern Europeans also tend to have somewhat
      more pigmented skin (Fitzpatrick III–IV) than northern Europeans, needing more UVB for the same
      synthesis, and sunscreen use is high. Add air-conditioned indoor lifestyles and you get a
      population that lives beside the sun without absorbing much of it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the studies find</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Spanish cohorts, particularly of older adults and postmenopausal women, repeatedly report high
      rates of 25(OH)D below 20 ng/mL, with winter troughs. Deficiency is well documented in the elderly
      and in institutionalised populations. Spain, like most of southern Europe, does not fortify foods
      broadly, so dietary vitamin D is low and cannot compensate. The result is that "we live in Spain,
      we get plenty of sun" is one of the least reliable assumptions in nutrition.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Don't assume Spanish sunshine equals sufficiency — behaviour and season matter more.</li>
      <li>Older adults and postmenopausal women are the priority groups for testing and supplements.</li>
      <li>A little non-midday sun on exposed skin in the warmer months helps; deliberate midday exposure is not worth the skin risk.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      For the wider reasoning, see our page on
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-hot-climates" class="text-amber-700 underline">hot and sunny climates</a>.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> The Mediterranean paradox is real — sunny surroundings do not
      guarantee good vitamin D status. If you are older or at risk, ask your médico about a test.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-spain" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
